@* A code sample: a header naming the language (and, when it matters, the file
   the sample belongs in), a copy button, and the highlighted sample itself.

   The highlighting is done in C# (CodeBlock.razor.cs) rather than by a script
   after render, for three reasons: the colours are in the prerendered HTML, so
   a crawler and a reader with JavaScript off both get them; there is no
   highlighting library to download on a site whose whole subject is what it
   costs to download an app; and nothing has to re-run when Blazor replaces the
   subtree on a client-side navigation.

   The markup below is written with no whitespace between <pre>, <code> and the
   sample, because inside a <pre> every space and newline in the template is
   content. *@

<div class="code-block">
    <div class="code-block-header">
        <div class="code-block-meta">
            <span class="code-block-lang">@Language</span>
            @if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Title) is false)
            {
                <span class="code-block-title">@Title</span>
            }
        </div>

        @* The button carries the accessible name, so both glyphs stay decorative;
           the label is a polite live region so the confirmation is announced and
           not only seen. Colour is never the only signal - the icon changes to a
           check and the word changes to "Copied". *@
        <button type="button" class="code-block-copy" data-demo-action="copy-code"
                aria-label="@($"Copy the {Language} sample to the clipboard")">
            <Icon Name="copy" Class="code-block-copy-icon" />
            <Icon Name="checkmark" Class="code-block-copy-check" />
            <span class="code-block-copy-label" aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
    </div>
    <pre><code>@_html</code></pre>
</div>
